.TH CJSON_DELETE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_delete \- remove and free the DOM node at a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_delete(cjson root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Expands to a call to _cjson_delete(root, path).

Navigates to the parent of the addressed node, removes the child, and
recursively frees its entire subtree.  The path uses the same
dot-separated syntax as cjson_get and cjson_set.

For dictionary parents, the leaf component is used as the key to
remove.  For list parents, the leaf component must be a '#N' token
(e.g. "#2" to remove index 2).

The split is performed on the LAST unescaped dot, so "a.b.c" targets
key "c" inside the node at "a.b".  When path contains no dot, root is
the parent.

Escape sequences '\e.' and '\e\e' produce a literal dot or backslash in
a key name, consistent with cjson_get and cjson_set.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t.  Returns ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if root or path is NULL, an empty path or path
component (leading, trailing, or consecutive dots), the leaf
component is not valid for the parent type, or a malformed "#N" index.
ccol_key_not_found if the parent path, or a syntactically valid leaf
key / index, is absent.
ccol_not_enough_memory on internal allocation failure.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
cjson_delete(doc, "users.#0.address");
cjson_delete(doc, "config.debug");
cjson_delete(doc, "items.#2");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_get (3),
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR _cjson_delete (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cjson_list_remove (3)
